# width: 120
# height: 40
# env: SHELL=/bin/sh
# `integrations.arrange` (`src/app/arrange.zig`): ONE rule for every
# pane that opens as a split — an integration, a terminal, a session
# pane. `.context`, the shipped default, reads the room. The first
# terminal takes an EMPTY editor area whole: nothing is split against a
# placeholder. Each one after that evens out every pane along the row,
# so two are halves and three are thirds. Before the rule the new pane
# halved whatever had the focus, and the third terminal got a quarter of
# the screen — `split_arrange_fixed.test` is that layout, still reachable
# on `.fixed`.
#
# The divider COLUMNS are the proof, and a blank row of the panes is how
# a script reads them: `toTestText` pads every row to the full width, so
# each expectation below is one whole 120-cell row, dividers and all.
# `/bin/sh` keeps the prompt to one line, well clear of them.
command view.toggle_tree

# One terminal, the whole area: there is no divider on the screen at all.
command term.shell
expect pane "Terminal (sh)"
expect screen lacks "│"

# Two: halves, the divider at column 60.
command term.shell
expect screen contains "▌                                                           │▌                                                          "

# Three: thirds, the dividers at columns 39 and 80 — not 60 and 90.
command term.shell
expect screen contains "▌                                      │▌                                       │▌                                      "
expect screen lacks "▌                                                           │▌                                                          "
expect screen lacks "▌                                                           │▌                            │▌                            "
